docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R10.log:145:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HwWQP7Vmw4eRLABr9z6wMU","type":"tool_result","content":"580\t\n581\t- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n582\t- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n583\t- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n584\t- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n585\t\n586\tIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n587\t\n588\tVerification:\n589\t\n590\t- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n591\t- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n592\t\n593\t### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n594\t\n595\tIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n596\t\n597\tDeliverables:\n598\t\n599\t- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n600\t- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n601\t- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n602\t- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n603\t- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n604\t\n605\tDefinition of done:\n606\t\n607\t- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n608\t- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n609\t- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n610\t\n611\t### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n612\t\n613\tDeliverables:\n614\t\n615\t- event model\n616\t- append-only SQLite event tables\n617\t- event writer API\n618\t- event reader/query API\n619\t- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n620\t- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n621\t- workflow task/session correlation\n622\t- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n623\t\n624\tDefinition of done:\n625\t\n626\t- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n627\t- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n628\t- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n629\t- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n630\t\n631\t### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n632\t\n633\tDeliverables:\n634\t\n635\t- redesigned memory schema\n636\t- first-class memory links\n637\t- first-class memory evidence\n638\t- memory access/use history\n639\t- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n640\t- memory stream/type taxonomy\n641\t- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n642\t- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n643\t\n644\tDefinition of done:\n645\t\n646\t- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n647\t- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n648\t\n649\t### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n650\t\n651\tDeliverables:\n652\t\n653\t- task intent classifier\n654\t- anchor extractor and resolver\n655\t- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n656\t- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n657\t- compact response shaper\n658\t- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n659\t- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n660\t\n661\tScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n662\t\n663\tDefinition of done:\n664\t\n665\t- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n666\t- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n667\t- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n668\t\n669\t### Phase 5: Working Memory\n670\t\n671\tDeliverables:\n672\t\n673\t- explicit per-task working memory state\n674\t- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n675\t- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n676\t- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n677\t- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n678\t\n679\tDefinition of done:\n680\t\n681\t- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n682\t- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n683\t\n684\t### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n685\t\n686\tDeliverables:\n687\t\n688\t- session consolidation jobs\n689\t- episode summary generation\n690\t- memory promotion rules\n691\t- duplicate detection\n692\t- contradiction detection\n693\t- supersession proposal\n694\t- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n695\t- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n696\t- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n697\t- replay-safe job execution\n698\t\n699\tDefinition of done:\n700\t\n701\t- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n702\t- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n703\t- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n704\t\n705\t### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n706\t\n707\tDeliverables:\n708\t\n709\t- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n710\t- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n711\t- exact-span evidence validation where available\n712\t- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n713\t- time-bound expiry\n714\t- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n715\t- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n716\t\n717\tDefinition of done:\n718\t\n719\t- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n720\t- verification results are explainable and queryable\n721\t\n722\t### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n723\t\n724\tDeliverables:\n725\t\n726\t- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n727\t- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n728\t- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n729\t- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n730\t\n731\tDefinition of done:\n732\t\n733\t- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n734\t- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n735\t\n736\t### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n737\t\n738\tDeliverables:\n739\t\n740\t- retrieval relevance metrics\n741\t- memory usefulness metrics\n742\t- context token efficiency metrics\n743\t- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n744\t- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n745\t- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n746\t- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n747\t- regression dashboard or CLI report\n748\t\n749\tRequired metrics:\n750\t\n751\t- tool calls per successful task\n752\t- irrelevant files opened per task\n753\t- relevant anchor recall\n754\t- memory inclusion precision\n755\t- memory later-used rate\n756\t- stale memory surfaced rate\n757\t- contradiction missed rate\n758\t- tests recommended versus tests needed\n759\t- workflow success after first plan\n760\t\n761\tDefinition of done:\n762\t\n763\t- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n764\t\n765\t### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n766\t\n767\tDeliverables:\n768\t\n769\t- VS Code or local web review UI\n770\t- memory inbox\n771\t- promotion queue\n772\t- contradiction queue\n773\t- stale memory view\n774\t- event trace view\n775\t- retrieval explanation view\n776\t- consolidation job health\n777\t- indexing health\n778\t- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n779\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"beca6473-0a8b-4b3f-8c29-18a74c665d8f","uuid":"f41ba05f-8a78-460e-ae93-cffbe341e48a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:28:05.714Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md","content":"\n- the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild\n- the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data\n- the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage\n- two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases\n\nIf none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\n\nVerification:\n\n- current Lattice daemon tests pass in the fork before major changes\n- current MCP workflow fixtures run unchanged or with documented compatibility shims\n\n### Phase 1: Unified Identity Model\n\nIdentity must precede the event log. An event that references a file, symbol, or doc by unstable name cannot be reliably replayed or used for consolidation. This phase establishes the identity substrate that all subsequent phases depend on.\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- stable ids for files, symbols, docs, sections, events, memories, and context handles\n- identity resolver for paths, symbols, headings, tests, and event refs\n- explicit identity serialization in MCP payloads\n- compatibility support for legacy name-based references\n- tests for duplicate symbol names, renamed files, moved sections, and branch changes\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every workflow output can be expanded through a stable identity\n- ambiguous names return ambiguity diagnostics instead of arbitrary matches\n- identity resolution adds no more than 2ms P99 to hot-path tool calls\n\n### Phase 2: Event Log Substrate\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- event model\n- append-only SQLite event tables\n- event writer API\n- event reader/query API\n- event payload hashing and optional payload spillover\n- MCP/tool-call event capture using stable identities from Phase 1\n- workflow task/session correlation\n- tests for ordering, replay, corruption handling, and workspace scoping\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- every high-level MCP workflow records task, retrieval, response, and outcome events\n- event log can replay enough data to reconstruct workflow history\n- failure paths emit useful events without hiding errors\n- event write adds no more than 5ms P99 to hot-path tool calls (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`); large payloads spill to a side table rather than blocking the writer\n\n### Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned memory schema\n- first-class memory links\n- first-class memory evidence\n- memory access/use history\n- contradiction and supersession as graph edges\n- memory stream/type taxonomy\n- migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\n- tests for memory creation, linking, contradiction, supersession, stale state, and replay\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\n- every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\n\n### Phase 4: Retrieval V1\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- task intent classifier\n- anchor extractor and resolver\n- hybrid candidate retrieval from graph, docs, memories, and events\n- scoring model with graph proximity, semantic similarity, freshness, scope, verification, and usefulness\n- compact response shaper\n- diagnostic response mode with ranking reasons\n- benchmark suite for realistic assistant tasks\n\nScope constraint: Phase 4 retrieval must not depend on the formal working memory state model defined in Phase 5. Retrieval returns bundles into the calling context; working memory formalization is layered on top in Phase 5. Design the retrieval output schema to be forward-compatible with working memory checkpoints without requiring a Phase 4 rewrite.\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- memory retrieval no longer depends on keyword search as the primary path\n- workflow tools return memory with inclusion reasons and stable expansion handles\n- irrelevant memory rate is measured and regressed\n\n### Phase 5: Working Memory\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- explicit per-task working memory state\n- operations for retrieve, summarize, filter, pin, evict, expand, compress, and checkpoint\n- MCP surface for inspecting current working memory\n- automatic event capture for included and excluded context\n- tests for token budgets, pinned context, eviction, and checkpoint restore\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- workflow tools can show what context is active, why it is active, and what was intentionally excluded\n- context management is no longer just prompt accumulation\n\n### Phase 6: Consolidation Engine\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- session consolidation jobs\n- episode summary generation\n- memory promotion rules\n- duplicate detection\n- contradiction detection\n- supersession proposal\n- procedure extraction from repeated successful workflows\n- failure-pattern extraction from repeated diagnostics\n- manual review queue for high-scope memory changes\n- replay-safe job execution\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- completed tasks produce useful episode memories\n- repeated successful traces produce procedural memories\n- consolidation is auditable, reversible, and test-covered\n\n### Phase 7: Verification And Freshness\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- incremental memory verification against graph changes\n- file/symbol/doc/test existence checks\n- exact-span evidence validation where available\n- branch and workspace scope enforcement\n- time-bound expiry\n- stale memory surfacing in workflow bundles\n- verification jobs and operator diagnostics\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- stale or contradicted memory cannot appear as normal trusted guidance\n- verification results are explainable and queryable\n\n### Phase 8: Workflow Engine V2\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- redesigned versions of context, change planning, scenario tracing, failure diagnosis, docs retrieval, test selection, impact analysis, and playbook generation\n- workflow composition over graph, event, memory, and working-memory substrates\n- one-call edit-planning bundles with code, docs, tests, memory, risks, and verification commands\n- workflow outcome recording integrated by default\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- common coding tasks require fewer manual discovery calls\n- workflows produce enough context to implement and verify changes without broad file dumping\n\n### Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- retrieval relevance metrics\n- memory usefulness metrics\n- context token efficiency metrics\n- stale/contradicted memory surfacing metrics\n- test-pass-after-plan metrics\n- repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\n- regression dashboard or CLI report\n\nRequired metrics:\n\n- tool calls per successful task\n- irrelevant files opened per task\n- relevant anchor recall\n- memory inclusion precision\n- memory later-used rate\n- stale memory surfaced rate\n- contradiction missed rate\n- tests recommended versus tests needed\n- workflow success after first plan\n\nDefinition of done:\n\n- changes to retrieval, memory, and workflow behavior can be evaluated without relying on anecdote\n\n### Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX\n\nDeliverables:\n\n- VS Code or local web review UI\n- memory inbox\n- promotion queue\n- contradiction queue\n- stale memory view\n- event trace view\n- retrieval explanation view\n- consolidation job health\n- indexing health\n- one-click accept/reject for memory evolution proposals\n","numLines":200,"startLine":580,"totalLines":969}}}
docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/logs/R10.log:202:{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N6HjjchNEH3jQq2X6L9rhP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# T11 — Phase 2 — Event model types (all 20 event kinds + envelope)\n2\t\n3\t**Phase:** 2\n4\t**Type:** backend\n5\t**Model class:** balanced\n6\t**Depends on:** R10\n7\t**Spec anchor:** [§Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log)\n8\t**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n9\t\n10\t## Goal\n11\t\n12\tThis task introduces the Rust type system for the append-only event log substrate. It defines the `EventKind` enum covering all 20 spec event types, the shared `EventEnvelope` struct carrying workspace id, branch, session id, task id, actor, timestamp, stable references, payload hash, compact summary, and optional full payload location, plus the typed payload structs for each kind. Without these types, Phase 2 storage, writer, reader, and capture work has nothing to serialize. Every later phase (memory consolidation, verification, retrieval, metrics) depends on these typed records, so getting the model right now prevents downstream churn.\n13\t\n14\t## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n15\t\n16\t> \"Required event types: `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ToolResult`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `PlanCreated`, `FileRead`, `PatchApplied`, `TestRunStarted`, `TestRunCompleted`, `DiagnosticObserved`, `UserCorrection`, `UserPreferenceObserved`, `WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`, `MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryInvalidated`, `MemoryConsolidated`.\"\n17\t\n18\t> \"Events should carry: workspace id, branch, session id, task id, actor, timestamp, stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories, payload hash, compact payload summary, optional full payload location.\"\n19\t\n20\t> \"The full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\"\n21\t\n22\t## Inputs\n23\t\n24\t- Dependency outputs: R10 (Phase 1 unified-identity review) — events MUST reference files, symbols, docs, and prior events through the stable identity types defined in Phase 1, not through transient names.\n25\t- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n26\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` — current serde patterns for memory records.\n27\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — current serde + chrono conventions for SQLite timestamps.\n28\t  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` — current `ContextBundle`, `WorkflowOutcome` shapes that some event payloads will summarize.\n29\t  - Phase 1 identity crate (`lattice-identity` or `identity` module per T03 decision) — the source of `FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `EventId`, `MemoryId`, `ContextHandleId`.\n30\t- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Naming, §Schema / model parity, §No broken windows; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references for spec-bound behavior.\n31\t\n32\t## Steps\n33\t\n34\t1. Read spec §Event Log in full and copy the 20 event-kind list verbatim into a doc comment on `EventKind` so the enum is self-documenting.\n35\t2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/mod.rs` exporting the module surface (`kinds`, `envelope`) and a top-level doc comment that points to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Event Log.\n36\t3. In `events/kinds.rs`, define `pub enum EventKind` with the 20 variants in spec order: `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ToolResult`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `PlanCreated`, `FileRead`, `PatchApplied`, `TestRunStarted`, `TestRunCompleted`, `DiagnosticObserved`, `UserCorrection`, `UserPreferenceObserved`, `WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`, `MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryInvalidated`, `MemoryConsolidated`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`. Implement `EventKind::as_str()` returning the canonical snake_case wire name used in storage.\n37\t4. In `events/kinds.rs`, define one typed payload struct per variant (e.g. `AssistantTaskStartedPayload`, `ToolCalledPayload`, `ToolResultPayload`, `ContextBundleReturnedPayload`, `MemoryRetrievedPayload` …). Each payload references prior identity types (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `EventId`, `MemoryId`, `ContextHandleId`) — never raw paths or names.\n38\t5. Define `pub enum EventPayload` wrapping all 20 payload structs so the envelope can carry any kind without dynamic dispatch.\n39\t6. In `events/envelope.rs`, define `pub struct EventEnvelope` with the spec-required fields: `event_id: EventId`, `workspace_id: WorkspaceId`, `branch: BranchRef`, `session_id: SessionId`, `task_id: Option<TaskId>`, `actor: Actor`, `timestamp: chrono::DateTime<Utc>`, `kind: EventKind`, `references: Vec<StableRef>`, `payload_hash: PayloadHash`, `summary: CompactSummary`, `payload_location: PayloadLocation`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Debug`.\n40\t7. Define `StableRef` as an enum over `FileRef(FileId)`, `SymbolRef(SymbolId)`, `DocSectionRef(DocSectionId)`, `EventRef(EventId)`, `MemoryRef(MemoryId)`, `ContextHandleRef(ContextHandleId)` so every reference is identity-typed.\n41\t8. Define `PayloadLocation` as `Inline { bytes_len: u32 }` or `Spilled { row_id: i64 }` so T12 can decide spillover policy without changing the envelope.\n42\t9. Define `Actor` as `enum { Assistant { model: String }, User, Tool { name: String }, Daemon }`. Define `CompactSummary` as a newtype around `String` with a hard ceiling (e.g. 512 bytes) enforced at construction.\n43\t10. In `events/tests.rs`, write tests that round-trip each `EventKind` through serde, assert canonical wire names, and verify every payload struct serializes deterministically (sorted JSON keys) so payload hashing in T13 is stable.\n44\t\n45\t## Files expected\n46\t\n47\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/mod.rs` — module root; re-exports `EventEnvelope`, `EventKind`, `EventPayload`, `StableRef`, `Actor`, `CompactSummary`, `PayloadHash`, `PayloadLocation`; doc comment cites spec §Event Log.\n48\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/kinds.rs` — `EventKind` enum with all 20 variants, per-kind typed payload structs, `EventPayload` wrapper, serde derives.\n49\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/envelope.rs` — `EventEnvelope` plus supporting types (`Actor`, `StableRef`, `PayloadLocation`, `CompactSummary`, `PayloadHash`).\n50\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/tests.rs` — unit tests for serde round-trip per kind, canonical wire names, summary ceiling, deterministic key ordering.\n51\t\n52\t## Verification\n53\t\n54\t- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/mod.rs` — file exists at the path the harness expects.\n55\t- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/kinds.rs` — file exists at the path the harness expects.\n56\t- `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` — the new module compiles cleanly with the rest of the crate.\n57\t- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests` — every event-model unit test passes.\n58\t\n59\t## Coding-standard checks\n60\t\n61\t- File length ≤800 lines per file (split `kinds.rs` if it grows beyond, e.g. one sub-module per kind family).\n62\t- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n63\t- Schema parity: every variant has a typed payload struct with serde derives; every non-private field has an explicit type and doc comment.\n64\t- No silent error handling — `Result` returned from `CompactSummary::new` is checked at every call site or annotated.\n65\t- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, commented-out code.\n66\t- For SQLite: not applicable here (T12 owns SQL); ensure no schema bytes leak into this task.\n67\t- Use `tracing` only where construction failure must be observable; types themselves are passive.\n68\t\n69\t## Definition of done\n70\t\n71\t- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content.\n72\t- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n73\t- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n74\t- [ ] No deferred work: all 20 event kinds defined with payloads in this task; storage, writer, reader, capture, snapshot, and tests are sibling Phase 2 tasks (T12–T17), not deferrals.\n75\t- [ ] Architecture doc updated in the same task if behavior is publicly observable — covered by T16's `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-compaction.md` for compaction; this task adds the doc-comment heading reference to the spec §Event Log on `mod.rs` and `EventKind`.\n76\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"beca6473-0a8b-4b3f-8c29-18a74c665d8f","uuid":"14669d41-252f-4a46-b68f-ea54dba9acde","timestamp":"2026-05-17T04:33:08.509Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T11.md","content":"# T11 — Phase 2 — Event model types (all 20 event kinds + envelope)\n\n**Phase:** 2\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R10\n**Spec anchor:** [§Event Log](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#3-event-log)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task introduces the Rust type system for the append-only event log substrate. It defines the `EventKind` enum covering all 20 spec event types, the shared `EventEnvelope` struct carrying workspace id, branch, session id, task id, actor, timestamp, stable references, payload hash, compact summary, and optional full payload location, plus the typed payload structs for each kind. Without these types, Phase 2 storage, writer, reader, and capture work has nothing to serialize. Every later phase (memory consolidation, verification, retrieval, metrics) depends on these typed records, so getting the model right now prevents downstream churn.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required event types: `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ToolResult`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `PlanCreated`, `FileRead`, `PatchApplied`, `TestRunStarted`, `TestRunCompleted`, `DiagnosticObserved`, `UserCorrection`, `UserPreferenceObserved`, `WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`, `MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryInvalidated`, `MemoryConsolidated`.\"\n\n> \"Events should carry: workspace id, branch, session id, task id, actor, timestamp, stable references to files, symbols, docs, events, and memories, payload hash, compact payload summary, optional full payload location.\"\n\n> \"The full event stream should not be injected into prompts. It exists for retrieval, audit, replay, metrics, and consolidation.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R10 (Phase 1 unified-identity review) — events MUST reference files, symbols, docs, and prior events through the stable identity types defined in Phase 1, not through transient names.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` — current serde patterns for memory records.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — current serde + chrono conventions for SQLite timestamps.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/` — current `ContextBundle`, `WorkflowOutcome` shapes that some event payloads will summarize.\n  - Phase 1 identity crate (`lattice-identity` or `identity` module per T03 decision) — the source of `FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `EventId`, `MemoryId`, `ContextHandleId`.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Hard limits, §Naming, §Schema / model parity, §No broken windows; Lattice rules on Markdown heading references for spec-bound behavior.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Event Log in full and copy the 20 event-kind list verbatim into a doc comment on `EventKind` so the enum is self-documenting.\n2. Create `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/mod.rs` exporting the module surface (`kinds`, `envelope`) and a top-level doc comment that points to `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Event Log.\n3. In `events/kinds.rs`, define `pub enum EventKind` with the 20 variants in spec order: `AssistantTaskStarted`, `ToolCalled`, `ToolResult`, `ContextBundleReturned`, `MemoryRetrieved`, `MemoryExpanded`, `PlanCreated`, `FileRead`, `PatchApplied`, `TestRunStarted`, `TestRunCompleted`, `DiagnosticObserved`, `UserCorrection`, `UserPreferenceObserved`, `WorkflowSucceeded`, `WorkflowFailed`, `MemoryCreated`, `MemoryUpdated`, `MemoryInvalidated`, `MemoryConsolidated`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`. Implement `EventKind::as_str()` returning the canonical snake_case wire name used in storage.\n4. In `events/kinds.rs`, define one typed payload struct per variant (e.g. `AssistantTaskStartedPayload`, `ToolCalledPayload`, `ToolResultPayload`, `ContextBundleReturnedPayload`, `MemoryRetrievedPayload` …). Each payload references prior identity types (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `EventId`, `MemoryId`, `ContextHandleId`) — never raw paths or names.\n5. Define `pub enum EventPayload` wrapping all 20 payload structs so the envelope can carry any kind without dynamic dispatch.\n6. In `events/envelope.rs`, define `pub struct EventEnvelope` with the spec-required fields: `event_id: EventId`, `workspace_id: WorkspaceId`, `branch: BranchRef`, `session_id: SessionId`, `task_id: Option<TaskId>`, `actor: Actor`, `timestamp: chrono::DateTime<Utc>`, `kind: EventKind`, `references: Vec<StableRef>`, `payload_hash: PayloadHash`, `summary: CompactSummary`, `payload_location: PayloadLocation`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Debug`.\n7. Define `StableRef` as an enum over `FileRef(FileId)`, `SymbolRef(SymbolId)`, `DocSectionRef(DocSectionId)`, `EventRef(EventId)`, `MemoryRef(MemoryId)`, `ContextHandleRef(ContextHandleId)` so every reference is identity-typed.\n8. Define `PayloadLocation` as `Inline { bytes_len: u32 }` or `Spilled { row_id: i64 }` so T12 can decide spillover policy without changing the envelope.\n9. Define `Actor` as `enum { Assistant { model: String }, User, Tool { name: String }, Daemon }`. Define `CompactSummary` as a newtype around `String` with a hard ceiling (e.g. 512 bytes) enforced at construction.\n10. In `events/tests.rs`, write tests that round-trip each `EventKind` through serde, assert canonical wire names, and verify every payload struct serializes deterministically (sorted JSON keys) so payload hashing in T13 is stable.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/mod.rs` — module root; re-exports `EventEnvelope`, `EventKind`, `EventPayload`, `StableRef`, `Actor`, `CompactSummary`, `PayloadHash`, `PayloadLocation`; doc comment cites spec §Event Log.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/kinds.rs` — `EventKind` enum with all 20 variants, per-kind typed payload structs, `EventPayload` wrapper, serde derives.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/envelope.rs` — `EventEnvelope` plus supporting types (`Actor`, `StableRef`, `PayloadLocation`, `CompactSummary`, `PayloadHash`).\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/tests.rs` — unit tests for serde round-trip per kind, canonical wire names, summary ceiling, deterministic key ordering.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/mod.rs` — file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/events/kinds.rs` — file exists at the path the harness expects.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build -p lattice-core` — the new module compiles cleanly with the rest of the crate.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests` — every event-model unit test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split `kinds.rs` if it grows beyond, e.g. one sub-module per kind family).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: every variant has a typed payload struct with serde derives; every non-private field has an explicit type and doc comment.\n- No silent error handling — `Result` returned from `CompactSummary::new` is checked at every call site or annotated.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, commented-out code.\n- For SQLite: not applicable here (T12 owns SQL); ensure no schema bytes leak into this task.\n- Use `tracing` only where construction failure must be observable; types themselves are passive.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content.\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: all 20 event kinds defined with payloads in this task; storage, writer, reader, capture, snapshot, and tests are sibling Phase 2 tasks (T12–T17), not deferrals.\n- [ ] Architecture doc updated in the same task if behavior is publicly observable — covered by T16's `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-compaction.md` for compaction; this task adds the doc-comment heading reference to the spec §Event Log on `mod.rs` and `EventKind`.\n","numLines":76,"startLine":1,"totalLines":76}}}
